{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_c4wnan3ygomxtvy46on4kujch37cgr2mmgsoqyfpvrcg2be4tjfa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. All four phases passed.\n\n## Completion gate\n\n- [x] **`SYMBOLS.md`** — `nm -D` diff is **empty**: all 28 symbols the C `.so` exports are exported by the Rust `.so` under identical names. 0 unresolved non-libc imports. No C source was untranslated (`lib.c` is a single TU, fully covered).\n- [x] **Phase B** — all **100 `CONFIGS.md` rows** pass across fixed-seed randomized sweeps.\n- [x] **Phase C** — all **53 testable `ERRORS.md` rows** have passing error-path differential tests (rows 53/54 are unconditional null derefs in the C — documented, not comparable).\n- [x] **Every configuration** — `Cargo.toml` has no `[features]`, so the complete combination set is `{default}` ≡ `{--no-default-features}`; verified in both `dev` and `release`, plus against the C built at `-O0/-O1/-O2/-O3`.\n\n**139 tests, 0 failures, 0 compiler warnings** in every configuration.\n\n## What I found and fixed\n\nThe prior translation was already semantically faithful. Three real changes:\n\n1. **`c2RaytoPoly` pointer provenance** (`src/lib.rs`) — the C reads past `verts[8]`/`norms[8]` when `count > 8`. The Rust derived its cursors from the array *fields*, so those reads were outside the pointer's provenance. Now derived from the `c2Poly*` base via `offset_of!` byte arithmetic, preserving the caller's provenance. Verified with a padded aligned buffer for `count` 9–24.\n2. **Harness must `dlopen` libm with `RTLD_GLOBAL`** — the C `.so` imports `sqrtf`, which on glibc ≥ 2.34 lives only in `libm.so.6`; Rust's `--as-needed -lm` drops it from test binaries that don't use libm themselves, so `dlopen` failed with `undefined symbol: sqrtf`.\n3. Two of my own hand-computed expectations were wrong, not the code — corrected to the C's measured behavior (capsule side-crossing returns 1, and **`poly_ray` returns 0 with both out-params untouched**: both hard-coded rays miss the box).\n\n## One documented comparator relaxation\n\nOnly the **NaN payload/sign** is exempt from bit equality. On x86 SSE an op with two NaN operands returns the *destination* register's NaN, so the survivor is chosen by register allocation — GCC `-O0` even swaps operand order between the two `mulss`es of a single `c2Dot`. `tests/nan_payload.rs` proves this hides nothing:\n\n- **579,998** floats from finite inputs: **100% bit-identical**, no relaxation used.\n- **1,060,000** floats from full-spectrum inputs: 5,958 (0.56%) differ, and **every one has both sides NaN**. One-side-NaN or two differing non-NaN values fails the test.\n\nInteger returns, NaN-ness, `±0.0` and `±inf` are always compared exactly. Every raycast is also called three times per input with different poison patterns in the `c2Raycast` out-param, so *\"did the callee write?\"* is part of the comparison — that's what pins `c2RaytoCapsule`'s unconditional pre-write before `return 0`."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1460,"cache_read_input_tokens":239359,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1460,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c09e83d0-98c3-47f2-aeb0-21e30c5abbe5","uuid":"d3a68aa7-8975-4a97-b3ab-662a33d4fadd","timestamp":"2026-08-20T12:25:53.509Z"}
